ike and what you're like as a parent  and who you are in general maybe more  surprisingly or at least more  surprisingly to me  breastfeeding has been embraced by the  Christian right so for many on the  Christian Right breastfeeding turns out  to be proof of intelligent design and it  also offers evidence of the rightness of  heterosexual marriage with different  roles for men and women in in the  marriage and it's also been picked up as  an issue for environmentalists and for  people who believe in locally locally  produced sustainable food because after  all what could be more locally produced  than breast milk so for all of these  groups of people many of whom are  actually fundamentally opposed to one  another wouldn't see eye to eye on  practically any other issue across the  social and political spectrum and yet  they all believed in breastfeeding and  the point that I'm showing is that they  all believe in breastfeeding for  fundamentally different reasons they've  they've appropriated the symbol of  breastfeeding but for many many  different reasons this is this is the  the power point I was supposed to show  with the attachment parenting many of  you will remember this Time magazine  cover of this attachment parent  breastfeeding her son who's three or  four years old and maybe not  coincidentally wearing camouflage pants  I thought that was the most sort of  disquiet in part of the whole  sure was the camouflage pants all right  so that's the first I did actually um he  doesn't he looks old and big anyway so  that's the first part of the puzzle  that's the first part of the picture  which is the ways in which breastfeeding  has taken on symbolic meaning and how  much value and some symbolic value it  has for so many people breastfeeding  isn't all things to all people but it is  a surprising number of things to a  surprising number of people the second  part of the puzzle or the second piece  of the puzzle is actually has to do not  surprisingly with business there's a  there's a business angle to this which  is surprising to those of us who still  associate the business of infant feeding  with the business of the big formula  companies so the idea here is that  breastfeeding is free that was long the  challenge against breastfeeding and yet  it's actually far from free in 21st  century America and the first reason  it's far from free is that if you ever  plan to leave the house you're going to  have to buy some clothes and some bras  and you might need some nutritional  supplements to make sure that you have a  good milk supply and then you need  pillows and shields and creams and all  kinds of other things it's not necessary  to buy any of these things but most  people who breastfeed in the United  States today will buy many of these  things the second component of the  business of breastfeeding is lactation  consultants most women who breastfeed  will see a lactation consultant at some  point in their breastfeeding career and  that will run you anywhere from 200 to  500 dollars an hour so there's an entire  industry of lactation consultants who  are making their living off of  breastfeeding  and the final component of the business  of breastfeeding is the breast pump  industry in the United States in 2007  people did a really large study on  pumping and breastfeeding and they  discovered that 85% of American women  who breastfeed were also pumping that's  an enormous percentage of the population  and and it explains why 40 percent of  the world market for breast pumps is an  American market there's nowhere else in  the world where women are breast pumping  pumping breast milk as much as they are  in the United States and so in the next  few years the world market the world  breast pump market is expected to reach  2.6 billion dollars annually and as I  said 40 percent of that market is in the  United States and all of this pumping  creates something that is new in the  history of humankind which is a surplus  of 